WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:04.143
- Children deserve like
detailed, detailed stop motion.

00:00:05.133 --> 00:00:07.883
(relaxing music)

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:11.470
- As Kirsten Lapore,

00:00:11.470 --> 00:00:13.740
our animation director,

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:15.360
put it at a Q and A,

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:17.970
it actually takes so much more effort

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:21.300
to make things be invisible
and effortless. (laughs)

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:25.290
- She's just so one of a kind, incredible.

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:27.480
- People think it takes a long time,

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:30.810
but like, it really takes
a long time. (laughs)

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:31.710
Like I would say,

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:33.570
I would say, a good reference for people

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:35.901
to think about is like, you know,

00:00:35.901 --> 00:00:38.130
on an ideal day you might get,

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:38.970
you know, five,

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:40.500
maybe 10 seconds of footage.

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:41.910
And that's, like 10 seconds,

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:43.530
like that's a killer shoot day, you know,

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:45.780
one animator getting a ten second shot.

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:47.490
I think that's a good
sort of frame of reference

00:00:47.490 --> 00:00:49.650
for how labor intensive it is,

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:51.630
but also how much it sort of pays off,

00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:54.000
and you get the magic of the real objects.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:58.290
- To play this character
who is heavily improvised

00:00:58.290 --> 00:01:00.990
and then pair it with a process

00:01:00.990 --> 00:01:03.720
that at once has to be filled with life,

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:05.790
like you have to be completely open

00:01:05.790 --> 00:01:07.650
and also detail oriented,

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:09.720
down to the tiniest minutia,

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:12.480
in order to make Marcel
seem like his emotions

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:13.590
aren't just coming out of his mouth,

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:15.210
but that they're in his body.

00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:16.350
That's a real honor.

00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:18.133
It's like a real honor to pair,

00:01:18.133 --> 00:01:22.800
to have one of my performances
paired with that type of art,

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:27.800
because there is so much
dedication to that intention

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:30.000
and that feels really good.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.130
And it's actually something
we've tried for Marcel,

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:33.390
like in general, you know,

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:35.010
with our children's books,

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:38.850
all of the images in those
books are oil paintings

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:41.040
done by an oil painter named Amy Lind.

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:43.440
It's the same pairing that like,

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:45.930
I think the thinking
behind that has always been

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:47.940
that adults enjoy Marcel,

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:49.500
and children do too.

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:51.510
One thing people don't
often give to children

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:54.180
is like rich art, (laughs)

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:56.700
and like, we were like,
children deserve oil paintings.

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:59.010
They should just have
Marcel in them, you know?

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:01.530
Children deserve like detailed,

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:03.586
detailed stop motion.

00:02:03.586 --> 00:02:06.060
(soft music playing)

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:08.100
- [Kirsten] Dean was very
involved in the whole process.

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:10.050
I mean, we, from the beginning,

00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:12.300
like from the time the
audio play was ready

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:13.770
and all the voices were recorded,

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:16.410
we worked really closely together
story boarding everything.

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:18.480
So it was literally just us on Zoom,

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:20.070
basically back and forth,

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:21.450
just planning out every single shot

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:23.550
meticulously in board form.

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:25.590
So we had really our kind of whole roadmap

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:28.170
of of what to do once
we came to the studio

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:29.010
to actually shoot.

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:30.780
- [Dean] I mean, I think
your job as a director

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:33.750
is to prepare like a mad person

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:36.180
and be a total perfectionist
in the preparation.

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:37.470
And then, when you're actually on set,

00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:39.210
be willing to throw
everything out the window

00:02:39.210 --> 00:02:41.850
and just be an opportunist.

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:42.900
- [Kirsten] I mean, I was both on set

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:44.370
for the live action shoot,

00:02:44.370 --> 00:02:45.630
really involved in every shot,

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:47.460
making sure we're sort of timing out.

00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:49.680
Okay, how far are we moving Marcel?

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:50.844
You know, like in this very,

00:02:50.844 --> 00:02:52.494
you know, like in five seconds,

00:02:52.494 --> 00:02:53.880
how far would he move?

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:56.160
And I had kind of like a
little puppet on a stick

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:58.770
that I would approximate and
sort of like bop it along,

00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:02.520
and then once we got to
the stop motion shoot,

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:05.100
then we sort of translated
that to that stage,

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:07.830
and Dean was with me
on every single stage.

00:03:07.830 --> 00:03:10.080
We would pow-wow before each shot

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:11.940
and sort of like act
out like a lot of times,

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:12.930
like I would play Marcel,

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:14.430
he would play Connie,

00:03:14.430 --> 00:03:16.650
and we would just kind
of play off of each other

00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:17.483
and figure out,

00:03:17.483 --> 00:03:18.480
Okay what is the performance
that we're really

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:19.890
going for here,

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:21.810
before we even go in to
talk to the animators.

00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:23.520
And then once we game planned

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:25.350
and got on the same page, we would go in

00:03:25.350 --> 00:03:27.570
and we would usually just
act it out for the animators,

00:03:27.570 --> 00:03:29.730
sometimes also making a
reference video for them,

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:32.040
just so they have as much
information as possible

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:33.090
going into their shot,

00:03:33.090 --> 00:03:35.490
because it takes them,
you know, a whole day,

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:37.890
sometimes two days, to do an entire shot.

00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:40.380
So you wanna avoid retakes
as much as possible

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:41.790
because you don't want someone redoing

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:43.230
like two days of work where they've been

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:45.693
like painstakingly laboring
over a tiny puppet for,

00:03:45.693 --> 00:03:47.580
you know, so many hours.

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:49.980
- [Jenny] When Dean is
dedicated to an aesthetic,

00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:51.810
he does it all the way.

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:54.720
He is the first true artist

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:56.430
that I feel like I've ever met,

00:03:56.430 --> 00:03:57.810
besides my own parents.

00:03:57.810 --> 00:04:01.737
And then to see his work
replicated by this entire team,

00:04:01.737 --> 00:04:03.720
you know, it was incredible

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:05.520
that there was just still a connection,

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:07.350
even with all of these other people there.

00:04:07.350 --> 00:04:08.788
They all know who lives here.

00:04:08.788 --> 00:04:11.580
They all, like, they all care.

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:13.020
- Even in the board phase,

00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:15.407
like we had a lot of
fun coming up with like,

00:04:15.407 --> 00:04:18.720
okay, what does one that
comes to mind is like,

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:21.450
you know, what is Connie's
little meal like that we cut to?

00:04:21.450 --> 00:04:23.220
And it's like, okay,
it's a quarter with like

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:25.650
a generic goldfish cracker
and like a little piece

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:27.630
of like dust on top,
you know, for garnish.

00:04:27.630 --> 00:04:28.590
Like those little things,

00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:29.970
that was, there were,

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:32.280
there were tons of them,
tons of them, like,

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:36.630
and just finding what those
little jokey moments are,

00:04:36.630 --> 00:04:37.740
that play with scale,

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:39.990
was I think one of the most
fun parts of the process,

00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:42.090
also like down the line, once it came time

00:04:42.090 --> 00:04:44.190
to design Marcel's family members,

00:04:44.190 --> 00:04:46.170
like the extended community shells,

00:04:46.170 --> 00:04:48.030
we brought in another friend of ours,

00:04:48.030 --> 00:04:48.870
this woman Aco,

00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:50.790
and we all kind of sat together

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:52.596
and went shopping to all these like,

00:04:52.596 --> 00:04:54.690
crazy electronic stores

00:04:54.690 --> 00:04:58.200
and like really specific
niche places all over LA,

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:00.090
like this shell store that was amazing.

00:05:00.090 --> 00:05:02.220
And we just like collected tons of stuff

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:03.750
and then laid it out on the table,

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:05.160
and we just spent hours like,

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:08.190
designing little characters
out of the actual props.

00:05:08.190 --> 00:05:10.140
And I think that's, you
know, something that you

00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:12.270
wouldn't be able to
necessarily just figure out

00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:14.520
off the top of your head
as you're even boarding.

00:05:14.520 --> 00:05:16.770
Like we really try to be
as hands on as possible

00:05:16.770 --> 00:05:19.200
in finding those actual
real objects, you know,

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:21.390
in the spirit of Marcel,
he's an actual real show.

00:05:21.390 --> 00:05:22.350
That's one of the fun parts

00:05:22.350 --> 00:05:23.700
about doing it in stop motion too.

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:25.320
Like that's what makes this movie so much

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:27.330
like tied to this specific medium,

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:29.850
like it really can't be done in CG,

00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:31.727
like I feel like the thing
that makes it truly real

00:05:31.727 --> 00:05:35.280
and using these actual
real objects like that,

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:38.220
that is sort of at the
heart of stop motion

00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:40.316
and working with actual things.

00:05:40.316 --> 00:05:43.080
(soft music playing)

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:44.310
- [Dean] You have to surrender yourself

00:05:44.310 --> 00:05:45.300
to the current moment.

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:46.860
And this movie was a,

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:47.820
exercise in that,

00:05:47.820 --> 00:05:51.090
because the whole point
was to try to make it feel

00:05:51.090 --> 00:05:53.850
authentically spontaneous and documentary,

00:05:53.850 --> 00:05:57.180
but obviously when you're
telling a story with stop motion,

00:05:57.180 --> 00:05:58.668
live action hybrid, that is,

00:05:58.668 --> 00:06:01.410
has all these things going on, you can't,

00:06:01.410 --> 00:06:03.570
it's an illusion that that was

00:06:03.570 --> 00:06:05.220
in any way spontaneous. (laughs)

00:06:05.220 --> 00:06:07.080
- [Kirsten] There are really no films

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:08.550
that are shot like this.

00:06:08.550 --> 00:06:10.020
I mean, with the exception of like,

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:11.280
Monkeybone a long time ago.

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:12.600
Even that isn't a great comp

00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:14.190
because it's kind of like a bigger puppet.

00:06:14.190 --> 00:06:15.960
So we really had nothing to go off of

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:17.910
and how to sort of figure out
how do we shoot this movie?

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:18.840
How do we make it work?

00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:20.940
And how do we make it
really really naturalistic

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:23.220
and feel like, oh, Marcel was just,

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:25.140
we turned a video camera on
and we just captured him.

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:27.180
So that was, you know,
definitely a challenge.

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:29.460
There was a moment where
we were considering,

00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:32.430
okay maybe we actually
shoot it in the house

00:06:32.430 --> 00:06:35.280
and have the animators like
down on the floor, you know,

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:37.200
crouching like with the puppets,

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:40.290
maybe we control all the
lighting in the entire house

00:06:40.290 --> 00:06:41.880
and like flag off all the windows.

00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:43.230
So that was something we considered.

00:06:43.230 --> 00:06:45.510
But in the end I think
that would be a little too

00:06:45.510 --> 00:06:47.460
physically grueling for those animators

00:06:47.460 --> 00:06:49.740
cuz houses aren't built like
stop motion sets, you know,

00:06:49.740 --> 00:06:51.540
stop motion sets are
built very particularly

00:06:51.540 --> 00:06:52.800
so we can get underneath them,

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:54.360
you can like tie things down,

00:06:54.360 --> 00:06:56.670
really get access to
everything you need to

00:06:56.670 --> 00:06:58.251
on that miniature stage.

00:06:58.251 --> 00:07:00.540
So ultimately we ended
up going that direction

00:07:00.540 --> 00:07:03.870
but a lot of other tests
of crazy camera heads

00:07:03.870 --> 00:07:05.280
and recording, you know,

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:07.260
the cameras motion and
translating that to the stage.

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:08.460
There were so many things we played with

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:09.630
and then ultimately we figured,

00:07:09.630 --> 00:07:10.830
okay we have to shoot this twice. (laughs)

00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:13.590
And just sort of competent
in a more traditional way.

00:07:13.590 --> 00:07:14.760
- [Dean] I felt very committed to

00:07:14.760 --> 00:07:16.110
trying to make this as much

00:07:16.110 --> 00:07:17.910
like a real documentary as possible.

00:07:17.910 --> 00:07:21.240
And I'd never seen a quote
unquote documentary that actually

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:24.363
took its subject seriously
and treated them with dignity.

00:07:24.363 --> 00:07:28.800
And so it was kind of always a
question of, well, how do we,

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:29.970
how do we solve these problems?

00:07:29.970 --> 00:07:33.242
How does Marcel get, you know,
to his stockpile or whatever?

00:07:33.242 --> 00:07:36.403
How does he get from one room
to another quickly while also

00:07:36.403 --> 00:07:39.510
you know, trying to keep it
within the realm of possibility

00:07:39.510 --> 00:07:41.820
because with an animated
character, you know,

00:07:41.820 --> 00:07:43.650
you could make him fly or whatever.

00:07:43.650 --> 00:07:47.160
You're not bound by those live
action rules, but it, so I,

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:49.500
I think we were constantly
trying to gut check ourselves

00:07:49.500 --> 00:07:52.350
against what a real documentary
would look like and do.

00:07:52.350 --> 00:07:55.920
- [Jenny] In movies for
children, there will be sorrow

00:07:55.920 --> 00:07:58.930
there will be loneliness or fear

00:07:59.850 --> 00:08:02.430
but this is a movie for everyone.

00:08:02.430 --> 00:08:05.880
And I think one thing that I try to do

00:08:05.880 --> 00:08:07.050
not just as an artist,

00:08:07.050 --> 00:08:09.750
but as a person is to soothe

00:08:09.750 --> 00:08:12.090
the child that remains inside of me.

00:08:12.090 --> 00:08:15.150
And I've also learned
that now as a parent that,

00:08:15.150 --> 00:08:15.983
through your art

00:08:15.983 --> 00:08:17.850
and through your behavior
with other people,

00:08:17.850 --> 00:08:19.588
you can start to kind of like,

00:08:19.588 --> 00:08:22.890
if you want to sort of re-parent yourself.

00:08:22.890 --> 00:08:25.890
And a lot of that has to do

00:08:25.890 --> 00:08:30.420
with allowing for a big
variety of emotions to

00:08:30.420 --> 00:08:34.680
be held inside of even
the smallest individual.

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:36.600
And you know, for it's like,

00:08:36.600 --> 00:08:39.180
it would be easy to make
Marcel like, just be cute.

00:08:39.180 --> 00:08:42.810
But instead I think what Dean
and I really wanted to do,

00:08:42.810 --> 00:08:45.480
and we were like, well,
when it comes down to it,

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:48.000
when we're alone as Dean and Jenny, what,

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:49.230
what makes us feel good?

00:08:49.230 --> 00:08:51.720
What makes us feel satiated is,

00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:55.383
putting as many emotions
as possible into one place.

00:08:57.740 --> 00:09:00.823
(soft music playing)

